---
// HijriDate 1.5 (files dated 2005-02-04), DinarSoft's Hijri date for the Windows Mobile Today screen, read back from
// HijriPlugin.dll. Facts: research/dinarsoft/re/hijridate/ (HOW.md, PROOF.md), research/dinarsoft/site/.
import CodeStepper from '../components/toys/CodeStepper.astro';
import TodayScreen from '../components/toys/ds-hijridate/TodayScreen.astro';
import HijriLab from '../components/toys/ds-hijridate/HijriLab.astro';
import MeccaRule from '../components/toys/ds-hijridate/MeccaRule.astro';
import GlyphWall from '../components/toys/ds-hijridate/GlyphWall.astro';
import WordStrip from '../components/toys/ds-hijridate/WordStrip.astro';
import InkLab from '../components/toys/ds-hijridate/InkLab.astro';
import FileMap from '../components/toys/ds-hijridate/FileMap.astro';
import PollLog from '../components/toys/ds-hijridate/PollLog.astro';
import KeyMachine from '../components/toys/ds-hijridate/KeyMachine.astro';
import TrialFile from '../components/toys/ds-hijridate/TrialFile.astro';
import ThenNow from '../components/explain/ThenNow.astro';
import Deep from '../components/explain/Deep.astro';
import Listing from '../components/explain/Listing.astro';
import Term from '../components/explain/Term.astro';
import { MECCA, STRIP, INK, KEY, KEY_CODE } from '../components/toys/ds-hijridate/steps.ts';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<section id="hd-home" class="hd">
  <h2>Arabic on the home screen</h2>
  <p class="lead">In 1991 Yasser’s <a href={`${base}/arabart/`}>ARABART</a> taught a DOS PC to write Arabic. In 2003 he did it again on a phone. HijriDate put today’s Hijri date, in Arabic, on the Pocket PC’s home screen, right under the owner’s name and the unread mail.</p>
  <p>The <Term id="hd-hijri">Hijri calendar</Term> follows the moon, and it sets the dates of Ramadan, the two Eids and the Hajj. A Pocket PC knew only the Gregorian calendar, and an English one had no Arabic font at all. HijriDate brought both. It is a <Term id="hd-today-plugin">Today plug-in</Term>, the 2003 version of a home screen widget, and it cost $2.99.</p>
  <p>This is HijriDate rebuilt from its own file: its word pictures, its four settings pages, its icons and its calendar engine. Tap the clock icon to open the settings. Change the format, move the date a day, switch to English, or try another theme.</p>
  <TodayScreen id="hd-today" />
</section>

<section id="hd-moon" class="hd">
  <h2>The moon decides</h2>
  <p class="lead">A Hijri month starts when the new crescent is first seen after sunset. HijriDate works that out from the moon itself, for any date, in about a dozen lines of arithmetic.</p>
  <p>Pick a date. The steps below the answer are his, in his order. Count the days and estimate which new moon came last. Work out the exact moment it was born, and decide when it could first be seen. Then count months from a fixed starting point. Every number is the one his code computes. The engine on this page is a line-for-line copy of his, and it matches his original, running in an ARM emulator, on all 37,700 dates from 1700 to 2300.</p>
  <HijriLab id="hd-lab" />
  <p>Your browser ships four Islamic calendars, and they do not always agree with each other. One follows Saudi Arabia’s official tables, one estimates from the sky, and two use plain arithmetic. Muslims in different countries can start Ramadan a day apart for the same reason: the crescent is seen in one place before another. That is why HijriDate has an adjustment of up to two days either way. The date on the phone then matches the sighting announced where you live.</p>
  <Deep title="The new-moon formula, in his order">
    <p>The moment of a new moon is a steady beat of 29.53 days, plus 13 small corrections for the pull of the sun and the tilt and stretch of the moon’s orbit, minus a few seconds for the Earth’s slowing spin. His code runs them in this order, with these numbers:</p>
    <Listing title="tmoonphase (0x1020A8), as C" lang="c" lines={[
      { code: 'jd = 2415020.75933 + 29.53058868 * k', note: 'the steady beat' },
      { code: 'sa = 359.2242 + 29.10535608 * k', note: 'where the sun is' },
      { code: 'ma = 306.0253 + 385.81691806 * k', note: 'where the moon is' },
      { code: 'tf = 2 * (21.2964 + 390.67050646 * k)', note: 'its height above the Earth’s path' },
      { code: 'x  = (0.1734 - 0.000393 * t) * sin(sa)', note: 'the biggest correction' },
      { code: 'x -= 0.4068 * sin(ma)', note: 'and 11 more like it' },
      { code: 'x -= (0.41 + 1.2053 * t + 0.4992 * t * t) / 1440', note: 'the Earth’s spin' },
      { code: 'return jd + x' },
    ]} foot="k counts new moons from January 1900; t is k in centuries. Every add and multiply is a call into the system’s software math, because the phone’s chip had no hardware for fractions." />
  </Deep>
</section>

<section id="hd-mecca" class="hd">
  <h2>Six in the morning, Mecca time</h2>
  <p class="lead">One rule turns the moment of the new moon into the first day of the month, and it fits in one short routine.</p>
  <p>A crescent needs time to grow wide enough to see. A new moon born before 6:00 in the morning in Mecca is old enough to be seen at sunset that same day. The month then starts the next morning. Born any later, it is seen one evening later. Step through his routine with the new moon of September 2006, then drag the birth time yourself.</p>
  <CodeStepper id="hd-mecca-cs" title="HIJRIPLUGIN.DLL  visible()" asm={MECCA.asm} c={MECCA.c} steps={MECCA.steps}>
    <MeccaRule slot="visual" id="hd-mecca-vis" minutes={MECCA.minutes} dates={MECCA.dates} />
  </CodeStepper>
  <p>Every fraction here, the 0.5, the 24 and the 6, goes through the system’s <Term id="hd-soft-float">software floating point</Term>, one call per add. Today, crescent calculators weigh the moon’s age, its height and its distance from the sun at every city. HijriDate packed the idea into one comparison against the holiest city, so the whole calendar fits beside a clock on a 2003 phone.</p>
</section>

<section id="hd-words" class="hd">
  <h2>Forty-four words, drawn by hand</h2>
  <p class="lead">Arabic letters change shape with their neighbors, and in 2003 a Pocket PC could not join them. HijriDate never needs to. It carries every word it will ever show as a picture. There are 7 days, 12 Hijri months, 12 Gregorian months, 10 digits, a comma, a dash and a space.</p>
  <p>Each is 16 pixels tall and one bit a pixel, ink or paper. Tap one to see its pixels.</p>
  <GlyphWall id="hd-glyphs" />
  <p>To write a date, he lists the words in reading order, then joins their pictures into one strip. Arabic reads right to left, but its numbers read left to right, so the digits of each number go into the list last first. Step through it:</p>
  <CodeStepper id="hd-strip-cs" title="HIJRIPLUGIN.DLL  building the date" asm={STRIP.asm} c={STRIP.c} steps={STRIP.steps}>
    <WordStrip slot="visual" id="hd-strip-vis" />
  </CodeStepper>
  <p>Eight formats come out of the same 44 pictures, from الأحد ٢، رمضان ١٤٢٧ to ٠٢-٠٩-٢٧. Today your phone shapes Arabic from a font on the fly. A website that ships its icons as one sprite sheet, or a game that draws its score from digit pictures, is doing what HijriDate did.</p>
</section>

<section id="hd-ink" class="hd">
  <h2>Any wallpaper, any color</h2>
  <p class="lead">Owners picked their own Today themes: blue waves, photos, black. The word pictures are plain black on white. Yet on the phone they show in the theme’s own text color, with the wallpaper showing through every gap.</p>
  <p>He does it with <Term id="hd-bitblt">BitBlt</Term>, the Windows call that copies pixels, and three of its <Term id="hd-rop">raster operations</Term>: XOR, AND and OR. Two run once to color a copy of the word. Two run every time it is drawn. Pick a color and a wallpaper, then step through his code.</p>
  <CodeStepper id="hd-ink-cs" title="HIJRIPLUGIN.DLL  mask and paint" asm={INK.asm} c={INK.c} steps={INK.steps}>
    <InkLab slot="visual" id="hd-ink-vis" />
  </CodeStepper>
  <ThenNow era="2003 to 2005" rows={[
    { then: '<code>SRCAND</code> with the word, then <code>SRCPAINT</code> with a colored copy', now: 'CSS <code>mask-image</code> over a <code>background-color</code>', why: 'One picture says where, another says what color.' },
    { then: '<code>GetTextColor</code> on the Today screen', now: '<code>currentColor</code> in an SVG icon', why: 'The icon takes the color of the text around it.' },
    { then: 'A colored copy kept until the color changes', now: 'A memoized render', why: 'Do the work once, reuse it until the input changes.' },
  ]} />
</section>

<section id="hd-built" class="hd">
  <h2>One small file</h2>
  <p class="lead">All of HijriDate is one file, HijriPlugin.dll: 95,232 bytes. The Today screen loads it, gives it a strip of the screen, and calls it.</p>
  <p>A <Term id="hd-dll">DLL</Term> is a library other programs load. The Today screen asks this one for two functions by number: 240 draws the item, 241 runs its settings. The installer uses the very same file for its own setup steps. Tap a part of the file to see what is in it.</p>
  <FileMap id="hd-file" title="HijriPlugin.dll, version 1.5" at={1} parts={[
    { name: 'Header', bytes: 1024, hue: 250, what: 'Tells Windows Mobile this is an ARM library and where each part starts.' },
    { name: 'Code', bytes: 32768, hue: 220, what: 'Every routine: the moon, the calendar, the strip of words, the coloring, the settings pages and the key check. About 8,000 ARM instructions.' },
    { name: 'Word pictures', bytes: 6528, hue: 30, what: 'The 44 Arabic words and digits. Every Arabic date HijriDate can show is built from these.' },
    { name: 'Logos', bytes: 33428, hue: 10, what: 'Two DinarSoft logos for the About page: <b>DiNAR</b> and the Arabic <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">دينار</span>, each with a globe. These are the logos DinarSoft used before November 2006, kept only here.' },
    { name: 'Icons', bytes: 4728, hue: 60, what: 'The small clock beside the date, and the icons for the settings and the installer.' },
    { name: 'Settings pages', bytes: 3368, hue: 140, what: 'Seven dialog layouts: Main, Hijri, Gregorian, About, License Key and two more. The rebuilt settings above are drawn from these.' },
    { name: 'Version and index', bytes: 4172, hue: 170, what: 'The version block, 1.5, and the index that finds each picture and page by number.' },
    { name: 'Data', bytes: 5632, hue: 290, what: 'The English month names, his Babylonian month names, the tables of word numbers and month lengths, and the settings in memory.' },
    { name: 'Tables for Windows', bytes: 3584, hue: 320, what: 'The list of functions it offers: 240 and 241 for the Today screen, Install_Init and Install_Exit for the installer. Then a table that lets Windows walk back through calls, and the fix-ups for loading at any address.' },
  ]} />
  <p>A widget on a phone today asks the system to wake it on a schedule. The Today screen of 2003 asked every plug-in, every two seconds, whether it needed to redraw. HijriDate answers no, again and again, until the day changes. Drag the device clock across midnight:</p>
  <PollLog id="hd-poll" />
  <ThenNow era="2003 to 2005" rows={[
    { then: 'A Today plug-in: a DLL with functions 240 and 241', now: 'A WidgetKit widget on iPhone, an App Widget on Android', why: 'A small view the home screen hosts and refreshes.' },
    { then: '<code>WM_TODAYCUSTOM_QUERYREFRESHCACHE</code> every two seconds', now: 'A widget timeline that names its next update', why: 'Redraw only when the content changes.' },
    { then: 'Settings under <code>Software\\Microsoft\\today\\items\\Hijri Date</code>', now: 'UserDefaults or SharedPreferences', why: 'A small key-value store per app.' },
    { then: 'One broadcast after install, <code>WM_WININICHANGE</code>', now: 'A widget reload call', why: 'Tell the home screen to look again, no restart.' },
  ]} />
  <p>That last row is a quiet kindness. HijriDate’s installer ends by sending the Today screen one broadcast message, so the date appears at once, with no restart.</p>
  <Deep title="His redraw check, in ARM">
    <Listing title="HIJRIPLUGIN.DLL  0x103EBC" lines={[
      { off: '103ECC', code: 'bl   GetLocalTime', note: 'what day is it now?' },
      { off: '103EE0', code: 'cmp  r2,r3', note: 'the same day as the one drawn?' },
      { off: '103EF8', code: 'cmp  r2,r3', note: 'the same month?' },
      { off: '103F0C', code: 'cmp  r2,r3', note: 'the same year?' },
      { off: '103F1C', code: 'cmp  r3,#0', note: 'settings changed?' },
      { off: '103F24', code: 'mov  r0,#1', note: 'yes: redraw', hi: true },
      { off: '103F2C', code: 'pop  {r4,pc}', note: 'otherwise 0: nothing to do' },
    ]} />
  </Deep>
</section>

<section id="hd-key" class="hd">
  <h2>A key for each Pocket PC</h2>
  <p class="lead">HijriDate ran free for 15 days. To keep it, you bought a key for the Pocket PC Code shown on its License Key page. That key worked on that Pocket PC alone.</p>
  <p>The code comes from the <Term id="hd-device-id">device ID</Term>, a number unique to each Pocket PC, folded into 16 letters and digits. The key is made from the code by a shuffle in the style of <Term id="hd-rc4">RC4</Term>. 120 numbers are mixed by a secret word, then walked to turn each character of the code into a letter. The letter O never appears, so nobody confuses it with a zero. Step through it, or type any code to see its key.</p>
  <CodeStepper id="hd-key-cs" title="HIJRIPLUGIN.DLL  the key" asm={KEY.asm} c={KEY.c} steps={KEY.steps}>
    <KeyMachine slot="visual" id="hd-key-vis" pass="Hijri" code={KEY_CODE} />
  </CodeStepper>
  <p>To check a key, HijriDate runs the same function on its own code and compares the two. The key is stored in the <Term id="hd-registry">registry</Term>, so it survives a restart. Run in an ARM emulator, HijriDate’s own check routine accepted the key for each of 200 Pocket PCs and turned away all 1,000 near misses.</p>
  <p>The 15 days are counted from a tiny file the installer writes, <code>\Windows\inter.dat</code>. It holds the install date twice, in two disguises, nudges every second byte by one, and ends with a checksum. Change any byte and see what HijriDate makes of it:</p>
  <TrialFile id="hd-trial" />
  <p>In 2026, Yasser Awad released HijriDate as freeware. The <a href="#unlock">unlock section above</a> makes the key for your Pocket PC from its code, with the same function you just stepped through.</p>
</section>

